Answers
The persona (and constraints like "one sentence only") in the system message visibly change the tone, length, and style of the reply — without changing the user question at all.
On this model, zero-shot does still return NEUTRAL for the dented-box case — so it gets the label right. The real value of few-shot here is output-format control: with the examples the model returns the bare label (NEUTRAL); without them it tends to wrap the answer in an explanatory sentence. Few-shot is about format, not just correctness.
With "think step by step", the model reasons through the problem and is far more likely to get a multi-step answer right (e.g. the bat-and-ball 0.5). Plain zero-shot tends to blurt the tempting-but-wrong answer.
Each turn, append the new user message and the model's reply to your messages list and resend the whole list. That growing list is your conversation state — the only "memory" a stateless model has.
Pinning the format ("reply with ONLY the label") and temperature=0 both push toward a bare label; the few-shot examples are usually the strongest lever because they show the exact output shape. In practice you stack all three. The lesson: format problems are fixed by format prompting, not by adding more rules.
With the "say I don't know" out, the model declines the unanswerable question instead of fabricating — an honest, checkable signal. Remove the line and it tends to invent a fluent, confident, wrong answer. Grounding (give it the facts) plus an out is the cheapest hallucination guard you have before reaching for RAG.